output:
  ok: true
  language:
    name: rust
    title: Rust
  table:
    target: target/1
    language: rust
    title: What Rust provides
    version: 1.97.1
    for: python
    about: How Rust, its standard library and the crates it is commonly given provide what a program leans on, for
      each gift the ground catalogue names; and the points of weighing that are facts of Rust itself.
    points:
    - point: 1
      score: 3
      evidence: the Rust Foundation (since 2021, several member companies) and the project's own teams and RFC process;
        stable releases every six weeks since 1.0 in 2015.
    - point: 2
      score: 2
      evidence: the Rust Reference and the Ferrocene Language Specification (MIT or Apache-2.0; neither claims to
        be complete); one production compiler, rustc, others partial (gccrs, mrustc).
    - point: 3
      score: 3
      evidence: the stability promise of 1.0 and editions; cargo builds old crates with new compilers.
    - point: 7
      score: 3
      evidence: memory-safe without unsafe, data races refused by the compiler; a panic exits with status 101, so
        a program whose uncaught errors exit 1 catches them in main.
    - point: 8
      score: 2
      evidence: exhaustive matching on kinds, the type of each field and the borrow checker; positions and texts
        are the judge's to check.
    provides:
    - entry: whole-numbers-unbounded
      by: library
      what: num-bigint, or i128 where the program's numbers stay small
      note: the standard library has no unbounded whole number; i64 overflow panics in a debug build and wraps in
        a release build
      gap: num-bigint pinned in Cargo.lock and fetched once, or the numbers held in i128 with a refusal past it,
        ruled by the transconceiver
    - entry: number-from-text
      by: built-in
      what: str::parse::<i64> after trimming
      note: underscores between digits, signs and non-ASCII digits the original accepts are glue to write
    - entry: halves-round-to-even
      by: built-in
      what: f64::round_ties_even (since 1.77)
    - entry: division-gives-fraction
      by: built-in
      what: f64 division after converting both sides
    - entry: division-floors
      by: written
      what: a floor division of a few lines
      note: / and % truncate toward zero; div_euclid differs for a negative divisor
    - entry: fractions-are-doubles
      by: built-in
      what: f64
    - entry: fraction-from-text
      by: built-in
      what: str::parse::<f64> after trimming
      note: underscores the original reads are glue
    - entry: number-as-text
      by: written
      what: the printed form of a number around f64's Display
      note: 'Display gives the shortest digits but never an exponent, Debug writes 1e16: where the form switches
        to an exponent, and inf and nan, are written'
    - entry: conversions
      by: written
      what: each conversion the program makes, with the original's refusals
    - entry: value-kinds
      by: written
      what: the kind names the original prints, written as words
    - entry: text-length-in-code-points
      by: built-in
      what: str::chars().count()
    - entry: text-indexed-by-code-point
      by: built-in
      what: a Vec<char>
      note: a str indexes bytes; a position the program shows is counted in chars
    - entry: text-order-by-code-point
      by: built-in
      what: str's Ord
      note: UTF-8 bytes order as their code points do
    - entry: sort-is-stable
      by: built-in
      what: slice::sort_by, which is stable
    - entry: what-is-a-space
      by: written
      what: char::is_whitespace with U+001C to U+001F added
      note: the set is written out
    - entry: text-split-on-space
      by: built-in
      what: str::split with the written space test
    - entry: lines-end-at
      by: written
      what: a line splitter ending lines where the original does
      note: str::lines ends lines only at \n and \r\n
    - entry: text-case-mapping
      by: built-in
      what: str::to_lowercase, str::to_uppercase
      note: the full mapping, one character to two included
    - entry: text-replace
      by: built-in
      what: str::replace, str::replacen
    - entry: text-repeated
      by: built-in
      what: str::repeat
    - entry: code-points-by-number
      by: built-in
      what: char::from_u32
      note: it refuses surrogates
    - entry: printed-form
      by: written
      what: the original's printed form of texts, lists and maps
      note: Debug prints differently
    - entry: format-spec
      by: written
      what: the format fields the program uses, over format!
      note: format! has width, alignment and precision, no grouping
    - entry: percent-format
      by: written
      what: the percent fields the program uses
    - entry: format-string-fields
      by: written
      what: a reader of brace fields ({name.attr[key]!r:spec}, {{ escapes)
    - entry: truth-of-values
      by: written
      what: each test of truth written out
      note: 'Rust has no truth of values: a test names what it tests'
    - entry: boolean-operators-give-operands
      by: written
      what: an if that gives one operand or the other
    - entry: any-all-stop-early
      by: built-in
      what: Iterator::any, Iterator::all
    - entry: map-keeps-order
      by: written
      what: a Vec of pairs, replaced in place
      note: HashMap keeps no order; indexmap would do it as a library
    - entry: set-members
      by: built-in
      what: HashSet, BTreeSet
    - entry: set-order-varies
      by: built-in
      what: HashSet iteration
      note: its order varies from run to run as well; only a sort makes it repeatable
    - entry: membership-identity-then-equality
      by: written
      what: == on values, with the identity cases written
    - entry: records-compare-by-fields
      by: built-in
      what: derive(PartialEq)
    - entry: lists-and-zip
      by: built-in
      what: Iterator::zip, which stops at the shorter
    - entry: generators-lazy
      by: built-in
      what: an Iterator
    - entry: uncaught-error
      by: written
      what: main catches every error, prints a report and exits 1
      note: a panic exits 101
    - entry: error-text
      by: built-in
      what: Display of the error
    - entry: error-kinds
      by: written
      what: an enum of the program's errors, matched by kind
    - entry: exit-status-from-return
      by: built-in
      what: std::process::exit, ExitCode
    - entry: exit-with-message
      by: built-in
      what: eprintln! and std::process::exit(1)
    - entry: exit-status-whole-number
      by: built-in
      what: std::process::exit
    - entry: chained-errors
      by: built-in
      what: Error::source
      note: the report the original prints of a chain is written
    - entry: optional-library
      by: written
      what: a cargo feature with the part that needs the library behind it
    - entry: missing-key-error
      by: written
      what: the message naming the key
    - entry: program-arguments
      by: built-in
      what: std::env::args_os
    - entry: argparse-surface
      by: written
      what: an option reader with the original's usage line, errors and exit status 2
      note: the standard library has none; a crate (clap) prints its own messages
      gap: 'the option reader written (a few hundred lines): usage line, error messages, exit status 2'
    - entry: argparse-help
      by: written
      what: the help screen, laid out as the original lays it out
    - entry: argparse-version
      by: written
      what: the version option
    - entry: argparse-abbreviation
      by: written
      what: unambiguous prefixes of long options, in the option reader
    - entry: argparse-negative-numbers
      by: written
      what: a negative number taken as a value, in the option reader
    - entry: argparse-optional-positional
      by: written
      what: an optional value matched once, in the option reader
    - entry: argparse-subcommands
      by: written
      what: sub-commands, in the option reader
    - entry: environment-read
      by: built-in
      what: std::env::var_os
    - entry: clock-seconds
      by: built-in
      what: SystemTime::now() since UNIX_EPOCH
    - entry: local-time
      by: library
      what: a time-zone crate (jiff or chrono)
      note: the standard library has no local time
      gap: a time-zone crate pinned in Cargo.lock and fetched once
    - entry: terminal-width
      by: written
      what: COLUMNS, else 80
      note: the terminal's own size needs a binding to the system
    - entry: print-line-end
      by: built-in
      what: println!
    - entry: output-encoding
      by: built-in
      what: UTF-8 bytes written as they are
    - entry: json-written
      by: written
      what: a JSON writer with the original's spacing, escapes and number forms
      note: the standard library has no JSON; serde_json writes its own forms
    - entry: json-keys-sorted
      by: built-in
      what: a BTreeMap, whose keys come sorted
    - entry: json-read
      by: written
      what: a JSON reader
      note: the standard library has none; serde_json pinned would do
    - entry: csv-rows
      by: written
      what: a reader and writer of comma-separated rows
      note: the csv crate would do it as a library
    - entry: home-and-tilde
      by: built-in
      what: the HOME variable, read with std::env::var_os
      note: a leading ~ is expanded by a line of glue
    - entry: path-written
      by: built-in
      what: std::path::Path
    - entry: path-separator
      by: built-in
      what: std::path::MAIN_SEPARATOR
    - entry: path-resolved
      by: built-in
      what: std::path::absolute, std::fs::canonicalize
    - entry: path-relative
      by: written
      what: a relative path from the working folder
      note: the standard library has no such function
    - entry: path-kinds
      by: built-in
      what: std::fs::metadata, std::fs::symlink_metadata
    - entry: folder-walk
      by: written
      what: a walk over std::fs::read_dir
      note: walkdir would do it as a library
    - entry: make-folders
      by: built-in
      what: std::fs::create_dir_all
    - entry: file-created-mode
      by: built-in
      what: OpenOptions, 0o666 with the umask applied
    - entry: newlines-translated
      by: written
      what: \r\n and \r read as \n where the original reads text
      note: Rust translates no line ends
    - entry: text-decoding-strict
      by: built-in
      what: String::from_utf8
      note: the error the original raises is written
    - entry: bytes-decoded
      by: built-in
      what: Vec<u8> and String
    - entry: source-encodings
      by: written
      what: UTF-8 with or without its mark, latin-1, a cp1252 table and the coding line
    - entry: package-data
      by: built-in
      what: include_bytes!, include_str!
    - entry: glob-patterns
      by: written
      what: a glob where * matches any run, / included
    - entry: glob-case-per-platform
      by: written
      what: the glob, folding case where the platform does
    - entry: glob-case-sensitive
      by: written
      what: the same glob, case for case
    - entry: pattern-syntax
      by: written
      what: each pattern written as a small matcher with the same outcome
      note: the standard library has no regular expressions; the regex crate has no look-around
    - entry: pattern-unicode-classes
      by: written
      what: the Unicode classes in each matcher (char::is_alphanumeric and the written space test)
    - entry: pattern-flags
      by: written
      what: case folding, line anchors and dot-all in each matcher
    - entry: toml-read
      by: library
      what: the toml crate
      gap: the toml crate pinned in Cargo.lock and fetched once, or a reader written for the tables the program
        reads
    - entry: html5-entities
      by: written
      what: the table carried as data and looked up as the original does
    - entry: tree-text-and-tail
      by: written
      what: an element type with its text and tail
    - entry: source-parsed
      by: written
      what: a tokenizer and a parser of the input language's grammar, with its lines
      note: the crates that parse it are made for their own programs
      gap: the parser written, the largest piece (thousands of lines); a spike shows it can be done
    - entry: identifiers-normalised
      by: library
      what: the unicode-normalization crate (NFKC)
      note: its Unicode tables may be another version than the original's
      gap: unicode-normalization pinned in Cargo.lock and fetched once
    - entry: parser-messages
      by: written
      what: the parser's own messages
    - entry: type-comments
      by: written
      what: type comments read as tokens and parsed alone
      note: part of the parser
    - entry: tree-dump
      by: written
      what: a dump of the tree
    - entry: literal-truth
      by: written
      what: constant folding on the tree
    - entry: program-tree-nodes
      by: written
      what: the kinds of the tree as an enum
    - entry: runtime-version
      by: built-in
      what: the edition and rust-version of Cargo.toml
    - entry: run-as-module
      by: built-in
      what: one program, a cargo binary
    - entry: append-is-whole
      by: built-in
      what: OpenOptions::append with one write_all per line
      note: the same assumption as the original's, made of the platform
    - entry: pytest-conventions
      by: dropped
      what: 'nothing: cargo test reads its own names'
      note: the original's test runner's names mean nothing to the new program
  for: python
